# Which average effect are you even estimating?
next: {"title":"Nobody complies: intention-to-treat versus the effect of treatment","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/nobody-complies-intention-to-treat-versus-the-effect-of-treatment.cast"}

## Three Estimands, One Lottery
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_steps
with:
    title: Three estimands, one lottery
    steps:
      - {tex: \tau_i = Y_i(1) - Y_i(0), note: Observed for nobody.}
      - {tex: 'ATE = E[\tau_i]', note: All low-income Oregon adults}
      - {tex: 'ATT = E[\tau_i \mid D_i = 1]', note: 'The ~10,000 enrolled'}
      - {tex: 'ATU = E[\tau_i \mid D_i = 0]', note: Everyone still uninsured}

Before a state can price a Medicaid expansion it has to know whose effect it is buying — and this series shows that Oregon's lottery delivers at least three answers, starting from an individual effect observed for exactly zero people.
    draw title step_0 note_0

Average tau over every low-income Oregon adult — including the ninety thousand who never applied's neighbours who did not — and you have the ATE.
    draw step_1 note_1

Condition on D equals one, the roughly ten thousand who actually enrolled, and the same lottery yields the ATT instead.
    draw step_2 note_2

    pause 0.3

The ATU covers those still uninsured, and the ATE is only the population-weighted average of the two.
    draw step_3 note_3

An expansion that enrolls the never-applied is buying the ATU — so the policy-relevant number is the one the experiment reports least often.
    highlight step_2 step_3 effect glow delivery grave

## When ATT and ATE come apart
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_steps
with:
    title: ATE = π·ATT + (1−π)·ATU
    steps:
      - {tex: '\text{ATE}=E[Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)]', note: 'Everyone eligible, covered or not.'}
      - {tex: '\text{ATE}=\pi\,\text{ATT}+(1-\pi)\,\text{ATU}', note: π = share who take up D_i = 1}
      - {tex: '\text{ATE}-\text{ATT}=(1-\pi)\big(\text{ATU}-\text{ATT}\big)', note: Zero only if effects don't vary with take-up.}

Now that the lottery has separated our three estimands, one choice remains: which population average a state expanding Medicaid should actually be quoting.
    draw title step_0 note_0

The ATE is only a weighted average — a share π who take up coverage carrying the effect on the treated, the rest carrying the effect on the untreated.
    draw step_1 note_1

    pause 0.3

So the two coincide only under homogeneous effects, or when the gain from coverage is uncorrelated with who enrols — and Oregon's ninety thousand applicants were a self-selected slice: sicker, poorer, and considerably more motivated to sign up.
    draw step_2 note_2

Which makes that correlation the whole problem: the treated are exactly the people whose effect we have least reason to think is typical.
    highlight step_2 effect glow

An expansion buys coverage for those not yet enrolled — so the number it needs sits on the ATU side; the ATT is the effect on the ones it never had to persuade.
    mark mark target step_2 kind box
    delivery grave

## Internally valid, externally wrong
voice: female
level: advanced
use: generic_axes_diagram
with:
    title: Internally valid, externally wrong
    x_label: Individual treatment effect τᵢ = Yᵢ(1) − Yᵢ(0)
    y_label: Density of τᵢ in the population
    curves:
      - {id: oregon, label: Oregon lottery population, expression: 70*exp(-((x-32)^2)/180), color: supply}
      - {id: next, label: Next state's expansion population, expression: 52*exp(-((x-62)^2)/330), color: demand}
    vlines:
      - {x: 32, label: Oregon estimate}
      - {x: 62, label: What the next state would get}

Now that we have seen ATT and ATE come apart inside one population, here is the crack that decides whether a legislature elsewhere should act on Oregon at all: the lottery averages over the people Oregon actually drew — low-income uninsured adults, largely around Portland, with very thin prior access to care.
    draw axes x_label y_label curve_oregon label_oregon

For the mean of that distribution the estimate is unbiased, randomization guarantees it, and unbiasedness is a property of the estimator, not a promise about anywhere outside Oregon.
    draw vline_0

A different state expands to a different case mix, different baseline access, a different τᵢ distribution — so the policy-relevant mean sits over here, and no amount of internal validity drags it back.
    draw curve_next label_next vline_1

Across the three parts: the design, not the question, chooses the population — so name the population before you name the effect, and next time it narrows again, to those who actually take up the coverage they were offered.
    highlight vline_0 vline_1 effect glow delivery grave

    draw title

